<h1>Impluvium</h1>
<p class="nav"><a href="Atrium">Back to the atrium</a></p>
<p>The <b>impluvium</b> is the sunken part of the atrium, designed to carry away the rainwater coming through the compluvium of the roof. Usually made of marble, it was placed a few centimetres below the floor.</p>
<h2>Shape</h2>
<p>Most impluvia are shallow rectangles, a little longer than they are wide, set square to the opening in the roof above them so that the rain falls straight in.</p>
<p>The basin is rarely deeper than a hand's breadth. A deeper pool would have been a hazard in a room that people crossed in the dark.</p>
<h2>Materials</h2>
<p>Early basins were lined with packed clay and later with a hard mortar of lime and crushed tile, which kept the water from soaking into the floor.</p>
<p>In richer houses the edge was a kerb of cut stone, and the floor of the basin was laid with marble slabs or with a mosaic of small tiles.</p>
<h2>Water</h2>
<p>From the basin the water ran through a pipe or a channel into a cistern below the floor, where it was kept for washing and, once settled, for drinking.</p>
<p>A small opening at the lowest corner let the basin drain after a storm, so that only a thin film of water stood in it on most days.</p>
<h2>Light</h2>
<p>Because the roof was open above it, the impluvium was the brightest place in the house. The rooms around the atrium took their light from it.</p>
<p>On hot days the water and the stone kept the air of the atrium cooler than the street outside.</p>
<h2>Decoration</h2>
<p>Some basins held a small fountain or a statue at one end, and some had planting boxes along their sides.</p>
<p>The kerb was a natural place for a table, and a heavy stone table often stood at the end of the basin facing the entrance.</p>
<h2>Later houses</h2>
<p>In some later houses the opening in the roof was closed over, and the impluvium became a decorative pool, or was paved over altogether.</p>
<p>Where it survived, it was kept for the look of the room as much as for the water it gathered.</p>
